**Issue:** Removing the allowlist and always setting `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request `Origin` (or `*`) while keeping `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` lets any website read credentialed API responses. A page at `https://evil.example` can `fetch(api, { credentials: "include" })` and the browser will expose the body because ACAO echoes `https://evil.example` with ACAC true.

**Why the mobile rationale fails:** Native apps do not enforce CORS; they never needed this header. WebViews still send a real origin — add those origins to `ALLOWED` instead of reflecting arbitrary ones.

**Also broken:** when `origin` is null, `ACAO: *` + `ACAC: true` is invalid per the CORS spec and browsers reject it.

**Fix:** keep an explicit allowlist and only emit credentialed CORS headers for listed origins:

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set([
    "https://app.example.com",
    // add known mobile-webview origins here
  ]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
